WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:03.010
Film Courage: Are romantic comedies driven
by plot or character?

00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:09.120
Author Pamela Jaye Smith: As a genre that has three
parts, the plot is just there.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:14.090
You know, you get together, you get apart
and then you get back together.

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:18.530
So I don’t think it’s so much plot that
drives it as it is character.

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:24.619
That’s where we find our interest is in
the people and how diverse are they and how

00:00:24.619 --> 00:00:25.919
opposite are they?

00:00:25.919 --> 00:00:28.050
Or where are their similarities?

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:32.780
What is it that took them apart and how will
they possibly get back together and who is

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:36.379
helping them do that and who is against them
doing that?

00:00:36.379 --> 00:00:41.219
So it’s more about character I think in
a romantic comedy because it’s a genre and

00:00:41.219 --> 00:00:45.399
it has its tropes and it has its steps if
those aren’t happening then you don’t

00:00:45.399 --> 00:00:47.420
have a romantic comedy.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:52.499
So character is the most important thing and
that’s what you should be focusing on.

00:00:52.499 --> 00:00:56.859
Film Courage: Let’s talk about the character
archetypes because you have as I was saying

00:00:56.859 --> 00:01:01.430
earlier sort of the hero worshiper, you had
the type-A ambitious one who doesn’t have

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:07.590
time for a relationship, you might have sort
of the hot girl that no guy is good enough

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:10.410
for, or similar with a male character and
then the shy types.

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:15.500
What are some of the archetypes that we typically
see in romantic comedies, either as the friend

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:17.010
or the protagonist?

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:22.500
Pamela Jaye Smith: Some of the typical archetypes
and in the book I did [Pamela’s book ROMANTIC

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.350
COMEDIES: These Films Can Save Your Love Life!],
there’s a whole section on each movie about

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:29.830
the lover types and indeed a lot of what you
just mentioned.

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:32.390
There’s the cynical person.

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:34.200
Now that’s always a real good one.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:37.250
Somebody who has been burned and disappointed
in love.

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:43.060
So the cynic is a wonderful archetype and
I like that definition that a cynic is just

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:45.700
a wounded romantic.

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:51.810
So seeing that character change and go through
a character arc can be very satisfying to

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:52.810
us.

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:57.610
Also, the person who doesn’t even realize
they need love, the clueless.

00:01:57.610 --> 00:02:02.500
Now that’s different from being a cynic,
but the clueless person is just “Oh no,

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:04.430
everything is fine.

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:07.180
Maybe I’m in a loveless relationship, but
it’s convenient.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:08.180
It works.”

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:09.950
So they need to be waked up.

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:18.310
So they’re the archetype of the person who
needs to be awakened, that just kind of “What?

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:19.590
Wow!

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:20.870
Oh!”

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:26.590
And then you’ve got another good one is
the bossy person who is always right and always

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:32.670
in charge or always wants to be anyway.

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:39.030
And then the person who (and I think this
is so well done) in DOWN WITH LOVE where you’ve

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:45.380
got Ewan McGregor as the type-A, confident,
always in charge and you know he’s running

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:46.700
the show.

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:51.780
And of course all of the women love him and
he’s got many, many, many girlfriends.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:59.691
And then you have Renée Zellweger who is
the formerly, mousy secretary who (I love

00:02:59.691 --> 00:03:05.890
this and your writers I think will all just
roll their eyes) she says “Well, you weren’t

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:09.430
paying any attention to me and so I decided
I was just going to go off and I was going

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:15.290
to write a book and so I did and then it became
a best-seller (international) and then…”

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:21.410
I went to see it with some writers and we
all went “Oh, is that how it’s supposed

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:22.410
to work?

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:26.330
You just write something and it becomes an
instant, international best-seller.”

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:27.340
Okay!

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:33.709
So she was the person who was waking up, now
she was waking herself up.

00:03:33.709 --> 00:03:40.280
But bringing the two of them together made
a really dynamic energy exchange in that film

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:42.720
DOWN WITH LOVE.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:51.870
So the various archetypes, you can go from
the disappointed in love to the still grieving.

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:57.330
In MUST LOVE DOGS, Christopher Plummer has
a wonderful line and he’s a father who is

00:03:57.330 --> 00:04:02.880
a widower and he says “No, I know I’ve
had the love of my life.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:07.070
And there will never, ever be anyone to take
her place.

00:04:07.070 --> 00:04:13.690
But I’m just out there tap dancing as fast
as I can having some fun and hoping I won’t

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:16.310
notice all the time what I’ve lost.”

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:21.680
I mean it’s very sad but it’s also very
hopeful.

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:31.229
So many, many character types can play a part
in a romantic comedy and by choosing different

00:04:31.229 --> 00:04:35.589
types than what you would think, you can make
a much more interesting story.

